q 0.800 0.820 0.860 RG 0.80 w 0.980 0.980 0.990 rg 40.00 485.00 515.00 105.00 re B Q
BT /F2 9.5 Tf 0.150 0.200 0.350 rg 52.00 575.00 Td (Architectural Trade-Off of the Bridge Pattern:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.2 Tf 0.200 0.200 0.200 rg 52.00 560.00 Td (- Increased Upfront Structural Complexity: Introduces additional interfaces and indirection. In scenarios where only) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.2 Tf 0.200 0.200 0.200 rg 52.00 548.00 Td (  one implementation platform will ever exist, this introduces premature abstraction.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.2 Tf 0.200 0.200 0.200 rg 52.00 536.00 Td (- Minor Indirection Overhead: Each high-level call requires dynamic dispatch through the bridge reference. In modern JVMs,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.2 Tf 0.200 0.200 0.200 rg 52.00 524.00 Td (  monomorphic or bimorphic call sites are inlined by the JIT compiler, making runtime performance overhead negligible.) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.5 Tf 0.150 0.200 0.350 rg 52.00 528.00 Td (Key Differences: Bridge vs Adapter Pattern:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.2 Tf 0.200 0.200 0.200 rg 52.00 513.00 Td (- Design Intent: Bridge is an UP-FRONT architectural pattern designed to let abstraction and implementation vary) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.2 Tf 0.200 0.200 0.200 rg 52.00 501.00 Td (  independently before code is finalized. Adapter is a RETROSPECTIVE pattern used to make incompatible existing interfaces work together.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.2 Tf 0.200 0.200 0.200 rg 52.00 489.00 Td (- Structural Focus: Bridge connects two parallel class hierarchies via composition. Adapter wraps a single existing class/adaptee.) Tj ET
